var e={entries:JSON.parse(`{"assign":{"short":"Puts the value on the right into the name on the left.","simple":"Like writing a new number on a labelled box: whatever was inside before is gone.","deep":"Assignment, not a question: comparing is <code>==</code>. In a declaration such as <code>uint8_t r = 0;</code> the <code>=</code> gives the variable its first value (initialisation)."},"arithmetic":{"short":"Add, subtract and divide, like on a calculator.","simple":"School maths, with one surprise: dividing two whole numbers throws away the remainder, so <code>7 / 2</code> is <code>3</code>.","deep":"Integer division truncates towards zero; write <code>7 / 2.0f</code> to get <code>3.5</code>. Small types such as <code>uint8_t</code> are promoted to <code>int</code> before the maths, so <code>pos * 3</code> cannot overflow until it is stored back."},"modulo":{"short":"The remainder after dividing: <code>7 % 3</code> is <code>1</code>.","simple":"Like a clock face: count past 12 and you start again from 1. That makes <code>%</code> perfect for things that go round and round.","deep":"Works on whole numbers only (use <code>fmodf</code> for floats). With a negative left side the result is negative too, so <code>-1 % 6</code> is <code>-1</code>, not <code>5</code>."},"compound-assign":{"short":"Shorthand that updates a variable using its own value: <code>x += 5</code> means <code>x = x + 5</code>.","simple":"Like topping up a jar: you don't empty it and refill, you just add a bit more.","deep":"The left side is evaluated once, which matters when it is something like <code>buf_[i++]</code>. The result is converted back to the variable's type, so a <code>uint8_t</code> quietly wraps past 255."},"increment":{"short":"Adds one to (<code>++</code>) or takes one from (<code>--</code>) a variable.","simple":"A tally counter you click: one click, one more.","deep":"<code>i++</code> gives back the old value then adds one; <code>++i</code> adds first. In a <code>for</code> loop on its own they behave the same. An unsigned <code>uint8_t hue</code> wraps from 255 to 0, which the rainbow in lesson 01 relies on."},"compare":{"short":"Compare two values and answer <code>true</code> or <code>false</code>; after a type name, &lt; &gt; hold a type instead.","simple":"Questions with a yes/no answer: is it equal? is it smaller? Note the double <code>==</code>: a single <code>=</code> would change the value instead of asking.","deep":"Comparing signed with unsigned converts the signed side to unsigned, so <code>-1 &lt; 1u</code> is false; the compiler warns about it. In <code>std::vector&lt;Card&gt;</code> or <code>static_cast&lt;int&gt;</code> the angle brackets are template brackets, not comparisons."},"logic":{"short":"Combine yes/no answers: <code>&amp;&amp;</code> means and, <code>||</code> means or, <code>!</code> means not.","simple":"\\"If it's raining <b>and</b> I'm outside, open the umbrella.\\" <code>!</code> flips an answer: not raining.","deep":"Short-circuit: <code>a &amp;&amp; b</code> never looks at <code>b</code> when <code>a</code> is false, which is why <code>queue_ &amp;&amp; xQueueReceive(queue_, ...)</code> is safe with a null queue. After a type (<code>T &amp;&amp;</code>) the double ampersand means a movable reference instead."},"ternary":{"short":"A one-line if/else that picks a value: <code>test ? this : that</code>.","simple":"\\"Is the HUD on? Then say 'on', otherwise say 'off'.\\" A question with two possible answers.","deep":"Both branches must be convertible to one common type. Only the chosen branch is evaluated, so it is safe to guard a division or a pointer with it."},"shift":{"short":"Slide the bits of a number left or right; one step left doubles it, one step right halves it.","simple":"Like moving a number's digits along a row of light switches: everything slides over and a new 0 comes in at the end.","deep":"Used to pack and unpack bytes: <code>(r &amp; 0xF8) &lt;&lt; 8</code> puts red into the top 5 bits of an RGB565 colour. Shifting by the type's width or more is undefined behaviour."},"bit-ops":{"short":"Work on the single bits of a number: <code>|</code> sets bits, <code>^</code> flips them, <code>~</code> inverts them all.","simple":"Think of a number as a row of 8, 16 or 32 light switches. These operators flip chosen switches without touching the others.","deep":"<code>flags |= bit</code> turns a bit on, <code>flags &amp;= ~bit</code> turns it off, <code>flags ^= bit</code> toggles it. Register drivers like our MFRC522 live on these. ArduinoJson reuses <code>|</code> for a default value: <code>in[\\"type\\"] | \\"\\"</code>."},"scope":{"short":"Reaches inside a namespace, class or enum to find a name: <code>std::min</code> is the <code>min</code> that lives in <code>std</code>.","simple":"Like an address: <i>London::Baker Street</i>. Two streets can share a name as long as they are in different towns.","deep":"The scope resolution operator. <code>Button::Event::Click</code> walks two levels; a leading <code>::</code> (as in <code>::ble::activity()</code>) starts from the global namespace, so a local name can never shadow it."},"arrow":{"short":"Uses a member of the thing a pointer points to: <code>p->x</code> is the same as <code>(*p).x</code>.","simple":"A dot (<code>.</code>) talks to an object you hold; the arrow talks to an object you only have the address of.","deep":"Member access through a pointer. Web handlers get <code>AsyncWebServerRequest *req</code>, so it is <code>req->send(...)</code>. Following a <code>nullptr</code> crashes the ESP32 with a LoadProhibited or StoreProhibited panic, so pointers are checked first."},"maths":{"short":"Maths helpers from the C library: sine, cosine, square root, rounding, absolute value and friends.","simple":"The scientific buttons on a calculator, ready to use in code. Angles are in radians: a full turn is about 6.283.","deep":"The <code>f</code> versions (<code>sinf</code>, <code>sqrtf</code>) work on <code>float</code>, which the ESP32-S3's hardware FPU handles quickly; the <code>double</code> versions run in software and are several times slower. <code>lroundf</code> rounds to the nearest whole number."},"snprintf":{"short":"Builds a piece of text from a pattern and values, into a box of fixed size.","simple":"A fill-in-the-blanks form: <code>\\"%d/%d\\"</code> has two blanks, and the numbers you pass fill them in order.","deep":"<code>snprintf(buf, sizeof(buf), fmt, ...)</code> never writes past the buffer and always ends the text with a <code>'\\\\0'</code>; too-long text is cut off. Same <code>%</code> codes as <code>Serial.printf</code>: <code>%d</code> int, <code>%u</code> unsigned, <code>%lu</code> unsigned long, <code>%.1f</code> float, <code>%s</code> text, <code>%02X</code> hex."},"void":{"short":"Means \\"nothing\\": a <code>void</code> function does its job but hands no value back.","simple":"Like a doorbell: you press it and something happens, but it doesn't give you anything to hold.","deep":"As a return type it means no result. <code>void *</code> is a pointer to memory of unknown type, as in the <code>void *arg</code> that FreeRTOS tasks and interrupt handlers receive; it must be cast back before use."},"bool":{"short":"A yes/no value: either <code>true</code> or <code>false</code>.","simple":"A light switch: on or off, nothing in between.","deep":"Takes one byte. Numbers and pointers convert to <code>bool</code> automatically (zero and <code>nullptr</code> are false), which is why <code>if (buf_)</code> works. Arduino's <code>boolean</code> is the same type."},"true-false":{"short":"The two values a <code>bool</code> can hold.","simple":"Yes and no, written the way the compiler understands.","deep":"Keywords of type <code>bool</code>; converted to numbers they become 1 and 0. FreeRTOS uses its own <code>pdTRUE</code>/<code>pdFALSE</code> for the same idea."},"int":{"short":"A whole number that can be negative, the everyday default for counting.","simple":"A box for whole numbers like -3, 0 or 42. No fractions allowed.","deep":"On the ESP32 an <code>int</code> is 32 bits: about -2.1 billion to +2.1 billion. Its size differs between chips (16 bits on an Arduino Uno), which is why hardware code often prefers <code>int32_t</code> and friends that say their size."},"long":{"short":"A whole number type that is at least as big as <code>int</code>; on the ESP32 both are 32 bits.","simple":"Another box for whole numbers. On this chip it holds exactly what an <code>int</code> holds.","deep":"<code>long</code> is 32 bits on the Xtensa ESP32 and <code>long long</code> is 64. <code>millis()</code> returns <code>unsigned long</code>, so printing it needs <code>%lu</code>; the code often casts with <code>static_cast&lt;unsigned long&gt;</code> to make that match exact."},"unsigned":{"short":"A whole number that can't go below zero, so it reaches twice as high.","simple":"Like a car's odometer: it only counts up from 0, and past its last digit it rolls back round to 0.","deep":"Unsigned maths wraps around instead of overflowing: <code>0u - 1</code> is the biggest value. That wrap is exactly why <code>now - last >= interval</code> keeps working when <code>millis()</code> rolls over. <code>unsigned(x)</code> is a quick cast, handy for <code>%u</code> in printf."},"float":{"short":"A number with a decimal point, like <code>3.14</code> or <code>-0.5</code>.","simple":"A ruler with fine marks: good for measurements, but tiny rounding errors creep in, so never test two floats for exact equality.","deep":"32 bits, about 7 significant digits. The ESP32-S3 has a single-precision FPU, so <code>float</code> maths is fast; write constants as <code>0.5f</code> so the compiler doesn't switch to slow <code>double</code>."},"double":{"short":"A decimal number with about twice the precision of <code>float</code>.","simple":"The same ruler as <code>float</code>, with even finer marks, but slower to read on this chip.","deep":"64 bits, about 15 significant digits. The ESP32-S3 FPU only does single precision, so <code>double</code> is done in software: fine for an occasional calculation, too slow for every pixel or audio sample."},"char":{"short":"One character of text, stored as a small number; <code>const char *</code> is a whole piece of text.","simple":"A single letter tile from a word game. Line tiles up, end with an invisible stop tile <code>'\\\\0'</code>, and you have a word.","deep":"One byte. A C string is an array of <code>char</code> ending in <code>'\\\\0'</code>; <code>char text[32]</code> holds up to 31 letters. <code>\\"hello\\"</code> is a <code>const char *</code> to text kept in flash, while <code>'h'</code> (single quotes) is one <code>char</code>."},"size-t":{"short":"An unsigned whole number made for sizes and counts of things in memory.","simple":"The number on a tape measure for memory: how many bytes, how many items. It can never be negative.","deep":"32 bits on the ESP32, the type <code>sizeof</code> gives and <code>malloc</code> takes. Print it with <code>%u</code> after a cast, or <code>%zu</code>."},"uint8-t":{"short":"A whole number from 0 to 255: exactly one byte.","simple":"The smallest box: one byte, eight on/off switches. Perfect for a colour channel, a pin number or a brightness.","deep":"<code>u</code> = unsigned, <code>int</code>, <code>8</code> bits, <code>_t</code> = a type. Defined in <code>&lt;stdint.h&gt;</code> so its size is the same on every chip. Arduino's <code>byte</code> is the same type. Past 255 it wraps to 0."},"uint16-t":{"short":"A whole number from 0 to 65 535: two bytes.","simple":"A box twice the size of a byte. Every screen colour in these labs fits in one.","deep":"Unsigned, 16 bits. The ST7789 speaks RGB565: 5 bits red, 6 green, 5 blue packed into one <code>uint16_t</code>. Also used for BLE handles and 16-bit UUIDs."},"uint32-t":{"short":"A whole number from 0 to about 4.29 billion: four bytes.","simple":"A big box for big counts, like milliseconds since the board started.","deep":"Unsigned, 32 bits: the ESP32's natural word size, so reads and writes of it are single instructions. Times from <code>millis()</code> live in it and wrap after about 49.7 days."},"uint64-t":{"short":"A huge whole number, 0 to about 18 quintillion: eight bytes.","simple":"A box so big it never fills up in practice. The chip's 48-bit MAC address fits easily.","deep":"Unsigned, 64 bits. On a 32-bit CPU each operation takes a few instructions, and reading one shared between an interrupt and the main code is not a single step, so the labs keep shared timestamps at 32 bits."},"int8-t":{"short":"A small whole number from -128 to 127: one byte that can be negative.","simple":"A one-byte box with room for a minus sign. Good for things like Wi-Fi signal strength in dBm.","deep":"Signed, 8 bits, two's complement. BLE transmit power and RSSI values are reported as <code>int8_t</code>."},"int16-t":{"short":"A whole number from -32 768 to 32 767: two bytes that can be negative.","simple":"A two-byte box with a minus sign. Screen coordinates use it, so shapes can start just off the edge.","deep":"Signed, 16 bits. Adafruit GFX takes <code>int16_t</code> for x, y, width and height, and 16-bit audio samples from the microphone are <code>int16_t</code> too."},"int32-t":{"short":"A whole number from about -2.1 billion to +2.1 billion: four bytes that can be negative.","simple":"The same box as <code>int</code> on this chip, but its name promises the size on every chip.","deep":"Signed, 32 bits. The INMP441 microphone sends 24-bit samples inside 32-bit I2S slots, which are read as <code>int32_t</code> and then shifted down."},"int64-t":{"short":"A huge whole number that can be negative: eight bytes.","simple":"A box that never runs out for clocks counting microseconds.","deep":"Signed, 64 bits. <code>esp_timer_get_time()</code> returns <code>int64_t</code> microseconds since boot, which would take about 292 000 years to overflow."},"string-class":{"short":"Arduino's text type: a piece of text that can grow, shrink and be searched.","simple":"A stretchy notebook page for words: add to it, cut bits out, look for a word, without counting letters yourself.","deep":"Keeps its letters on the heap (short ones inline), so repeated <code>+</code> in a busy loop churns memory. The labs use <code>String</code> for serial commands and web replies and fixed <code>char</code> arrays where speed or interrupts matter. <code>String(42)</code> turns a number into text."},"auto":{"short":"Lets the compiler work out the type from the value you give it.","simple":"Like saying \\"put it in a box of the right size\\" instead of naming the box.","deep":"The type is still fixed at compile time, not dynamic. <code>auto *self = static_cast&lt;Button *&gt;(arg);</code> avoids writing <code>Button</code> twice; <code>auto</code> is also how lambdas are stored, since their type has no name."},"nullptr":{"short":"A pointer that points at nothing.","simple":"An empty address label: there is no house to visit, so check before you go.","deep":"The type-safe null pointer constant (C++11), replacing <code>NULL</code> and <code>0</code>. Allocation functions such as <code>heap_caps_malloc</code> return it on failure, and many ESP-IDF calls take it for \\"I don't need this\\"."},"if":{"short":"Runs the next block only when the test in brackets is true.","simple":"\\"If it's raining, take an umbrella.\\" When it isn't raining, the umbrella step is skipped.","deep":"The condition is converted to <code>bool</code>, so numbers and pointers work directly. A declaration in the test (<code>if (DeserializationError e = deserializeJson(...))</code>) keeps the variable inside the if."},"else":{"short":"The other path: runs when the <code>if</code> test was false.","simple":"\\"...otherwise, wear sunglasses.\\" Exactly one of the two paths happens.","deep":"<code>else if</code> is just an <code>else</code> whose block is another <code>if</code>, so chains are checked top to bottom and stop at the first true test."},"for":{"short":"Repeats a block a counted number of times, or once for each item in a list.","simple":"\\"For each of the 6 bytes, print it.\\" Start at 0, check you're not done, do the job, count up, repeat.","deep":"<code>for (init; test; step)</code>. The range form <code>for (const auto &amp;card : cards)</code> visits every element of an array or container without an index."},"while":{"short":"Repeats a block for as long as its test stays true.","simple":"\\"While there are letters in the post box, take one out.\\" When it's empty, stop.","deep":"The test runs before each round, so the body may run zero times. A <code>while</code> that never ends inside <code>loop()</code> starves the rest of the program; the labs read input with <code>while (Serial.available())</code>, which ends as soon as the buffer is empty."},"do-while":{"short":"Like <code>while</code>, but the block always runs at least once before the test.","simple":"Taste the soup, then decide whether it needs more salt. You always taste first.","deep":"The test sits after the body and needs a semicolon: <code>do { ... } while (cond);</code>. Handy for \\"pick a random value until it's a good one\\"."},"switch":{"short":"Picks one of several paths by comparing a value against fixed choices.","simple":"A lift panel: press 3 and you go to floor 3. Each <code>case</code> is one button.","deep":"Works on whole numbers and enums, not text. Execution falls through into the next case unless you <code>break</code> or <code>return</code>. With an <code>enum class</code> the compiler warns about cases you forgot."},"case":{"short":"One choice inside a <code>switch</code>: the code to run when the value matches.","simple":"One button on the lift panel, with its floor written next to it.","deep":"The label must be a compile-time constant. Several labels can share one body by stacking them; a body without <code>break</code> runs on into the next case."},"default":{"short":"In a <code>switch</code>, the path taken when no <code>case</code> matched.","simple":"The \\"anything else\\" button.","deep":"Optional and can sit anywhere in the switch. In a class, <code>= default</code> means something else: ask the compiler to write that constructor or operator for you."},"break":{"short":"Leaves the current loop or <code>switch</code> straight away.","simple":"An emergency exit: stop going round, carry on after the loop.","deep":"Only leaves the innermost loop or switch. Inside a switch that sits in a loop, <code>break</code> leaves the switch, not the loop."},"continue":{"short":"Skips the rest of this round of a loop and starts the next one.","simple":"\\"This letter isn't for me: put it aside and open the next.\\"","deep":"In a <code>for</code> loop the step (<code>i++</code>) still runs before the next test. Often used to skip bad data early instead of nesting <code>if</code>s."},"return":{"short":"Ends the function now, optionally handing a value back to whoever called it.","simple":"Handing back the finished homework: the job is done and here is the answer.","deep":"In a <code>void</code> function a bare <code>return;</code> just leaves early, a common way to bail out on bad input (a guard clause). Local variables are destroyed on the way out, which is how <code>std::lock_guard</code> unlocks."},"setup-loop":{"short":"Every Arduino program has these two: <code>setup()</code> runs once at power-on, <code>loop()</code> then runs forever.","simple":"Opening a shop: <code>setup()</code> unlocks the door and switches on the lights once; <code>loop()</code> serves customer after customer until closing time (power off).","deep":"The Arduino core's <code>main</code> starts a FreeRTOS task (\\"loopTask\\", core 1, 8 KB stack by default) that calls <code>setup()</code> then <code>loop()</code> over and over. Keep <code>loop()</code> quick and non-blocking: check <code>millis()</code> instead of long <code>delay()</code>s. A lab's <code>lab.loop()</code> is the shell's own per-round work."},"lambda":{"short":"A small function written right where it's needed, often passed to another function to call later.","simple":"A sticky note with instructions you hand to someone: \\"when a request for / arrives, do this\\".","deep":"<code>[capture](params) { body }</code>. The capture list says what it may use from around it: <code>[this]</code> the current object, <code>[&amp;]</code> everything by reference, <code>[]</code> nothing. Web routes, Wi-Fi events and sorting all take lambdas here."},"variadic":{"short":"Means \\"any number of extra arguments\\", like the values a printf pattern needs.","simple":"\\"...and so on\\": the function takes as many extras as you give it.","deep":"In <code>void warn(const char *fmt, ...)</code> the extras are read with <code>va_list</code> and forwarded to <code>vsnprintf</code>; the <code>[[gnu::format(printf, 2, 3)]]</code> attribute lets the compiler check them against the pattern. In a macro, <code>__VA_ARGS__</code> pastes them in."},"inline":{"short":"Allows a function or variable to be defined in a header that many files include.","simple":"A recipe printed in every cookbook that needs it: the kitchen agrees they are all the same recipe, not rivals.","deep":"Its real job is the one-definition rule: an <code>inline</code> function in a <code>.h</code> may appear in many <code>.cpp</code> files without a linker clash. Whether the call is actually inlined is up to the optimiser. <code>constexpr</code> functions and functions defined inside a class are inline automatically."},"operator":{"short":"Teaches a type what a symbol like <code>==</code>, <code>[]</code> or <code>+</code> should do with it.","simple":"Like teaching a new game piece the existing rules: now <code>a == b</code> works for your own kind of thing too.","deep":"<code>bool operator==(const Uid &amp;o) const</code> is just a function with a special name; <code>explicit operator bool()</code> lets an object be tested in an <code>if</code>."},"noexcept":{"short":"A promise that a function will never throw an exception.","simple":"A sign on the door: \\"no surprises in here\\".","deep":"If it throws anyway, <code>std::terminate</code> ends the program. Standard containers use it to decide whether moving an element is safe. Arduino-ESP32 builds with exceptions available, but the labs never throw."},"override":{"short":"<code>virtual</code> lets a child class replace a function; <code>override</code> marks the replacement.","simple":"A form with a blank: the library leaves \\"how to draw one pixel\\" for you to fill in, and <code>override</code> says \\"this is my answer to that blank\\".","deep":"Calls go through a table of function pointers (the vtable), so <code>Adafruit_GFX</code> code ends up calling our <code>FrameBuffer::drawPixel</code>. <code>override</code> makes the compiler check that a matching virtual exists. NimBLE callbacks such as <code>onConnect</code> work the same way."},"explicit":{"short":"Stops the compiler from silently turning one type into another through a constructor.","simple":"\\"Ask first\\": a number won't become a <code>Button</code> just because a <code>Button</code> can be built from one.","deep":"Without it, a one-argument constructor is an implicit conversion, so a function taking a <code>BootButton</code> could be called with a plain number. Also used on <code>operator bool</code>."},"constructor":{"short":"A special function with the class's own name that sets up a new object.","simple":"The assembly instructions that run once when a new thing comes out of the box.","deep":"No return type. The list after <code>:</code> (<code>: pin_(pin), name_(name)</code>) fills members before the body runs, in the order they are declared. A <code>~Name()</code> destructor runs when the object goes away, like <code>FrameBuffer</code> freeing its pixels."},"address-reference":{"short":"Three jobs: after a type it makes a reference (another name), before a variable it takes its address, between numbers it is bitwise AND.","simple":"A reference is a nickname: <code>uint8_t &amp;r</code> lets a function write straight into your box. <code>&amp;x</code> is the box's street address. <code>a &amp; b</code> keeps only the bits that are on in both.","deep":"<code>T &amp;</code> binds to an existing object and can't be null or re-pointed; <code>const T &amp;</code> passes big things without copying. <code>&amp;lock_</code> gives a <code>portMUX_TYPE *</code>. <code>x &amp; 0xFF</code> masks the low byte."},"pointer":{"short":"Three jobs: after a type it declares a pointer, before a pointer it follows it, between numbers it multiplies.","simple":"A pointer is a slip of paper with an address on it. <code>*p</code> means \\"go to that address and use what's there\\". Between two numbers, it's just times.","deep":"Pointers are 4 bytes on the ESP32. <code>uint16_t *buf_</code> holds where the pixels start; <code>buf_[i]</code> is the same as <code>*(buf_ + i)</code>. <code>const char *</code> is a pointer to text you may read but not change."},"array":{"short":"A fixed row of values of the same type, numbered from 0.","simple":"An egg box: a fixed number of slots, all the same shape. Slot numbers start at 0, so a 6-slot box has slots 0 to 5.","deep":"<code>char text[32]</code> reserves 32 bytes right there, with no heap. Nothing checks the index, so writing <code>text[32]</code> silently tramples whatever lives next; <code>sizeof(text)</code> gives the full size in bytes, which is why buffer calls pass it."},"const":{"short":"Marks a value as read-only: once set, the code may not change it.","simple":"A label written in pen, not pencil.","deep":"Unlike <code>constexpr</code>, the value can come from running code (<code>const uint32_t now = millis();</code>). After a member function's brackets, <code>const</code> promises the function won't change the object. <code>const char *p</code> protects the text; <code>char *const p</code> protects the pointer."},"static":{"short":"Keeps a variable alive for the whole program, or shares one copy between all objects of a class.","simple":"Inside a function it's a notebook that stays on the desk between visits, so <code>loop()</code> remembers its last value.","deep":"Three meanings. A <code>static</code> local is created once and survives between calls. A <code>static</code> member belongs to the class, not each object (the interrupt handler <code>onEdge</code> is static and gets the object through <code>arg</code>). At file level it makes a name private to that file."},"volatile":{"short":"Tells the compiler this value can change behind its back, so it must reread it every time.","simple":"A post box that can fill up while you're not looking: you must walk over and check, not assume it's still empty.","deep":"Needed for variables written by an interrupt and read by normal code. It is <b>not</b> a lock and not atomic: a 64-bit value can still be torn. For data shared between tasks prefer <code>std::atomic</code>, a mutex or a critical section."},"sizeof":{"short":"Tells you how many bytes a type or variable takes up.","simple":"Like reading the capacity printed on a jar before you pour anything in.","deep":"Worked out at compile time, it costs nothing at runtime. <code>sizeof(text)</code> of an array is its full byte size, but of a pointer it is just 4. <code>sizeof(arr) / sizeof(arr[0])</code> counts elements."},"new-delete":{"short":"<code>new</code> builds an object in free memory (the heap); <code>delete</code> destroys it and gives the memory back.","simple":"Renting a storage unit: <code>new</code> rents one, <code>delete</code> hands back the key. Forget to hand it back and the space is lost until reboot.","deep":"Each <code>new</code> needs exactly one <code>delete</code> (<code>new[]</code> needs <code>delete[]</code>). On the ESP32 plain <code>new</code> may land in internal RAM or PSRAM depending on size and build settings; to choose, use <code>heap_caps_malloc</code>. <code>= delete</code> on a function forbids it, such as copying."},"progmem":{"short":"Asks for data to stay in flash memory instead of being copied into RAM.","simple":"Leave the big book on the library shelf and read it there, instead of photocopying it onto your small desk.","deep":"Essential on AVR Arduinos; on the ESP32 it is defined as nothing, because <code>const</code> data already stays in memory-mapped flash. It is kept for clarity and portability, as with the web page in <code>kPage</code>."},"iram-attr":{"short":"Places a function in the chip's fast internal RAM, so it can run the instant an interrupt fires.","simple":"Keeping the fire extinguisher by the door instead of in the basement: it must be grabbable immediately.","deep":"Normal code runs from external flash through a cache, and the cache is switched off while flash is written (NVS, OTA). An interrupt handler living in flash would then crash the chip; <code>IRAM_ATTR</code> keeps it in IRAM. Anything it calls should be in IRAM too, so keep handlers tiny."},"c-strings":{"short":"C's tools for text in <code>char</code> arrays: copy it, compare it, measure it.","simple":"Working with letter tiles by hand: copy a word onto your row, compare two rows, count the tiles before the stop tile.","deep":"<code>strlcpy(dst, src, sizeof(dst))</code> copies safely and always ends with <code>'\\\\0'</code>. <code>strcmp</code> returns 0 when two texts are equal (not true!), so code reads <code>strcmp(a, b) == 0</code>. <code>strlen</code> counts letters, not the final <code>'\\\\0'</code>."},"memcpy":{"short":"Copy, fill or compare a block of raw bytes.","simple":"Moving a whole tray of eggs at once instead of one egg at a time.","deep":"They work on bytes and know nothing about types: the length is always in bytes, so use <code>sizeof</code>. <code>memcpy</code> must not overlap (use <code>memmove</code>); <code>memcmp</code> returns 0 for equal. Card UIDs and BLE payloads are handled this way."},"struct":{"short":"Bundles several named values into one new type.","simple":"A form with labelled fields: one Card has a UID, a name and a colour, and they travel together.","deep":"Identical to <code>class</code> except members are <code>public</code> by default. The labs use structs for plain data (<code>HttpsResult</code>, <code>Frame</code>) and classes for things with behaviour."},"class":{"short":"Defines a new kind of object: the data it keeps and the things it can do.","simple":"A blueprint for a gadget: what parts it has inside and which buttons are on the outside.","deep":"Members are <code>private</code> by default. <code>class FrameBuffer : public Adafruit_GFX</code> inherits everything a GFX drawing surface can do. <code>enum class</code> reuses the word for something different: a scoped enum."},"enum":{"short":"A list of named choices, like <code>Click</code>, <code>DoubleClick</code>, <code>LongPress</code>.","simple":"A multiple-choice question: the answer must be one of the options printed on the sheet.","deep":"<code>enum class Event { ... }</code> is scoped (written <code>Event::Click</code>) and won't turn into an <code>int</code> by accident. <code>: uint8_t</code> picks the storage type. A plain <code>enum</code> leaks its names into the surrounding scope."},"namespace":{"short":"A named folder for functions and types, so names from different parts never clash.","simple":"Surnames for code: <code>pal::ink</code> and <code>sv::ink</code> can both exist, like two people called Sam in different families.","deep":"An unnamed <code>namespace { }</code> makes everything inside private to that <code>.cpp</code> file (internal linkage), as lesson 01 does with its helpers. <code>using namespace x;</code> pulls names out again; avoid it in headers."},"std":{"short":"The namespace of the C++ standard library: <code>std::min</code>, <code>std::vector</code>, <code>std::mutex</code>...","simple":"The toolbox that comes with every C++ compiler. <code>std::</code> says \\"take this tool from the standard box\\".","deep":"Arduino-ESP32 ships a full C++ standard library (GCC's libstdc++), including containers, algorithms, <code>&lt;atomic&gt;</code> and <code>&lt;mutex&gt;</code> on top of FreeRTOS. Watch heap use: containers allocate."},"access":{"short":"Decide who may use a class's members: everyone, only the class itself, or the class and its children.","simple":"The buttons on the front of a radio are <code>public</code>; the circuit board inside is <code>private</code>.","deep":"A class keeps its data <code>private</code> (here with a trailing <code>_</code>, as in <code>pin_</code>) and offers <code>public</code> functions, so it can change its insides without breaking callers. In <code>: public Adafruit_GFX</code> the word sets how the parent's members are inherited."},"this":{"short":"Inside a member function, a pointer to the object the function is working on.","simple":"\\"Me\\": the object pointing at itself.","deep":"Usually implicit (<code>pin_</code> means <code>this->pin_</code>). Passed explicitly when a C-style callback needs to find its way back, as in <code>attachInterruptArg(..., onEdge, this, CHANGE)</code> or a task's parameter, and captured by lambdas with <code>[this]</code>."},"using":{"short":"Gives a type a shorter or clearer name.","simple":"A nickname: say <code>JsonFn</code> instead of the whole long type every time.","deep":"<code>using JsonFn = std::function&lt;...&gt;;</code> is the modern form of <code>typedef</code> and reads left to right. <code>using namespace std;</code> and <code>using Base::f;</code> are other uses of the same word."},"union":{"short":"Several members sharing the same bytes, so only one is meaningful at a time.","simple":"One parking space with several possible cars: whichever parked last is the one that's there.","deep":"The union is as big as its largest member. Reading a member other than the one last written is technically undefined in C++ (GCC allows it); <code>memcpy</code> or <code>std::bit_cast</code> are the clean ways to reinterpret bytes."},"extern":{"short":"Says a variable exists, but is defined somewhere else in the program.","simple":"\\"The key is in the other room\\": you can talk about it here, and the linker fetches it later.","deep":"A declaration without a definition. The HTTPS client uses it to reach the Mozilla certificate bundle that the build links into flash, by its linker symbol names."},"constexpr":{"short":"A value fixed when the program is compiled, so it can never change while the board runs.","simple":"Like a number printed on the box instead of written on a sticky note: settled before the board ever powers up.","deep":"<code>constexpr</code> variables are computed by the compiler and cost no RAM at runtime; a <code>constexpr</code> function such as <code>rgb()</code> can run at compile time when its inputs are constants. The labs name these <code>kSomething</code>."},"static-cast":{"short":"Converts a value to another type on purpose, where you can see it.","simple":"Pouring a drink into a different glass, deliberately and in plain sight.","deep":"Checked by the compiler and free at runtime for number types. Common here: <code>static_cast&lt;unsigned long&gt;</code> to match <code>%lu</code>, and turning a <code>void *</code> back into the object it was. The short form <code>uint8_t(x)</code> does the same for simple types."},"reinterpret-cast":{"short":"Treats the same bytes as a completely different type, without changing them.","simple":"Reading a page of numbers as if it were letters: the ink is the same, only how you read it changes.","deep":"No conversion happens; it just relabels a pointer (for example <code>uint8_t *</code> to <code>const char *</code> for a byte buffer of text). Easy to misuse: alignment and strict aliasing rules still apply."},"const-cast":{"short":"Removes <code>const</code> from a pointer so it can be passed to an old-style function.","simple":"Borrowing a pen-written label for a function that only accepts pencil ones: you promise it won't actually write.","deep":"Only safe when the object was not really const to begin with, or when the callee does not modify it (many C APIs just forgot <code>const</code>). Writing through it to truly const data is undefined."},"template":{"short":"Writes a function or class once for any type; the compiler makes a copy for each type used.","simple":"A cookie cutter: one shape, any dough. <code>T</code> stands in for the dough until you choose it.","deep":"<code>template &lt;typename F&gt; void withChip(F &amp;&amp;fn)</code> accepts any callable, including lambdas, with no runtime cost. Every new type means a new compiled copy, so heavy use grows the firmware. Angle brackets supply the type: <code>std::vector&lt;Card&gt;</code>."},"std-min-max":{"short":"Give back the smaller or the larger of two values.","simple":"\\"Whichever is lower\\" and \\"whichever is higher\\": handy for keeping numbers in range.","deep":"Both arguments must be the same type, or you name it: <code>std::min&lt;uint32_t&gt;(n, 320)</code>. <code>std::max(0, n - 9)</code> stops a value going below zero."},"std-clamp":{"short":"Squeezes a value into a range: below the low end gives the low end, above the high end gives the high end.","simple":"A child gate on the stairs: you can move around, but not past the gates at either end.","deep":"C++17, from <code>&lt;algorithm&gt;</code>. All three arguments must share one type, hence the <code>0.0f</code> and <code>1.0f</code>. Arduino's <code>constrain()</code> does the same job."},"std-algorithms":{"short":"Ready-made loops from the standard library: fill, copy, sort, find the biggest...","simple":"Kitchen gadgets: you could chop by hand, but the gadget is quicker and doesn't slip.","deep":"From <code>&lt;algorithm&gt;</code>, they work on any range given as a start and an end pointer (or iterator), with the end one past the last item. <code>std::fill_n(buf_, n, c)</code> paints a whole screen; <code>std::sort</code> takes a lambda saying what \\"comes first\\" means."},"std-move":{"short":"Hands an object's contents over to another instead of copying them.","simple":"Moving house with the boxes as they are, instead of buying a copy of everything for the new place.","deep":"It is only a cast to an rvalue reference (<code>T &amp;&amp;</code>); the actual move is done by the receiving type's move constructor. Afterwards the source is valid but empty-ish, so don't rely on its value. Cheap for <code>String</code>, <code>std::vector</code> and <code>std::function</code>."},"std-vector":{"short":"A list that can grow and shrink, holding items of one type.","simple":"An expanding file folder: add sheets and it makes room, and you can still say \\"give me sheet 3\\".","deep":"Stores items in one contiguous heap block and doubles it when full, which moves (and may fragment) memory. The labs fill vectors outside time-critical paths, for example copying the card list for the screen. <code>v.size()</code>, <code>v.push_back(x)</code>, <code>v[i]</code>."},"std-function":{"short":"A box that can hold any function or lambda with a given shape, to call later.","simple":"A slot for a sticky note of instructions: any note fits, as long as it takes the same inputs.","deep":"<code>std::function&lt;void(Card &amp;)&gt;</code> takes a <code>Card &amp;</code> and returns nothing. Flexible but heavier than a template: calling it is indirect and large captures go on the heap. Good for callbacks stored in a class."},"std-atomic":{"short":"A variable that two tasks can read and write at the same time without ever seeing half a change.","simple":"A single light switch everybody can flip: no matter who touches it, it's always clearly on or off.","deep":"<code>std::atomic&lt;bool&gt;</code> and <code>std::atomic&lt;uint32_t&gt;</code> are lock-free on the ESP32. Use them for one small value such as a flag or counter; for several values that must change together, use a mutex."},"memory-order":{"short":"Says how strictly an atomic read or write must line up with the memory around it.","simple":"\\"Release\\" is like posting a letter only after it's sealed; \\"acquire\\" is opening it before reading. Together they guarantee the reader sees everything the writer prepared.","deep":"<code>relaxed</code> only makes the one value atomic. A <code>release</code> store paired with an <code>acquire</code> load makes everything written before the store visible after the load, across the ESP32's two cores. The default (<code>seq_cst</code>) is the strictest."},"std-mutex":{"short":"A lock that lets only one task at a time into a piece of code or data.","simple":"The single key to a shared bathroom: whoever holds it goes in, everyone else waits by the door.","deep":"On Arduino-ESP32 it is built on a FreeRTOS mutex, so a waiting task sleeps instead of spinning. Never lock it in an interrupt. Lock it through <code>std::lock_guard</code> so it is always unlocked, even on an early <code>return</code>."},"lock-guard":{"short":"Locks a mutex when created and unlocks it automatically at the end of the block.","simple":"A self-returning key: it takes the bathroom key as you go in and puts it back by itself when you leave, however you leave.","deep":"RAII: the constructor calls <code>lock()</code>, the destructor calls <code>unlock()</code> when the variable goes out of scope. That makes it impossible to forget the unlock on an early <code>return</code>. Keep the block short."},"decltype":{"short":"Asks the compiler \\"what type is this expression?\\" and uses the answer as a type.","simple":"\\"Give me a box exactly like that one.\\"","deep":"Evaluated at compile time; the expression is never run. Useful in templates and for naming a lambda's type."},"static-assert":{"short":"A check that runs while compiling; if it fails, the build stops with your message.","simple":"A gatekeeper at the factory: a part that doesn't fit never leaves the building.","deep":"Costs nothing at runtime. Typical use: check that a packed struct really has the byte size a protocol expects, or that a table has one entry per enum value."},"mutable":{"short":"Lets a member change even inside a <code>const</code> function, for bookkeeping that isn't part of the object's value.","simple":"A scratch pad clipped to a sealed document: the document stays the same, the notes can change.","deep":"Typical for a mutex or a cache inside an object whose read functions are <code>const</code>: locking changes the mutex, but not what the object means. On a lambda, <code>mutable</code> lets it change its own captured copies."},"include":{"short":"Pastes another file in here, so this file can use what it declares.","simple":"Like saying \\"see the attached recipe card\\" at the top of a recipe: the ingredients it lists are now available.","deep":"<code>&lt;Arduino.h&gt;</code> in angle brackets searches the library and system paths; <code>\\"pins.h\\"</code> in quotes looks next to this file (and the project's <code>include/</code>) first. It is plain text pasting, done before compiling."},"pragma-once":{"short":"Makes sure a header file is only pasted in once, however many files include it.","simple":"A \\"you've already read this\\" bookmark: the second time, the page is skipped.","deep":"Supported by GCC and every major compiler; it replaces the older <code>#ifndef X / #define X / #endif</code> include guard. Without it, a struct defined twice would be a compile error."},"define":{"short":"Creates a name that gets replaced by some text before the code is compiled.","simple":"Find-and-replace done by the computer just before reading your code: every <code>PIN_RGB</code> becomes <code>48</code>.","deep":"Macros know nothing about types or scope. <code>pins.h</code> uses them so a build flag like <code>-DPIN_RGB=38</code> can override a pin; for ordinary constants prefer <code>constexpr</code>. A macro with brackets, like <code>BLE_NOTE(kind, ...)</code>, pastes its arguments in."},"conditional-compile":{"short":"Keeps or drops lines of code before compiling, depending on whether a name is defined.","simple":"\\"If nobody chose a pin yet, use 48.\\" The lines that don't apply are cut out before the compiler ever sees them.","deep":"<code>#ifndef PIN_RGB</code> ... <code>#endif</code> lets <code>platformio.ini</code> build flags win over the defaults in <code>pins.h</code>. <code>#if</code> tests a constant expression; every <code>#if</code>/<code>#ifdef</code>/<code>#ifndef</code> needs a matching <code>#endif</code>."},"serial-begin":{"short":"Opens the serial line to your computer at a chosen speed, so the board can print messages.","simple":"Picking up the phone and agreeing how fast you'll both talk. 115200 is the speed the serial monitor listens at.","deep":"Speed in baud (bits per second); it must match <code>monitor_speed</code> in <code>platformio.ini</code>. In these labs <code>Serial</code> is UART0, reached through the USB-to-serial chip on the <code>COM</code> port, so nothing needs to wait for USB to enumerate."},"serial-printf":{"short":"Prints a line to the serial monitor, filling in values with <code>%</code> codes.","simple":"A form letter: <code>\\"%d cores\\"</code> has a blank, and the number you pass fills it.","deep":"Formats with <code>vsnprintf</code> then writes to UART0 at 115200 baud (about 11.5 KB/s, so long prints slow a loop down). Codes: <code>%d</code> int, <code>%u</code> unsigned, <code>%lu</code> unsigned long, <code>%.1f</code> float, <code>%s</code> C string (use <code>.c_str()</code> for a <code>String</code>), <code>%02X</code> two-digit hex. Add <code>\\\\n</code> yourself."},"serial-print":{"short":"Prints text or a number to the serial monitor; <code>println</code> also starts a new line.","simple":"Saying something out loud to your computer. <code>println</code> is like pressing Enter afterwards.","deep":"Accepts text, numbers and <code>String</code>; <code>Serial.print(x, HEX)</code> prints in hex. It writes into a transmit buffer and only waits when that buffer is full."},"serial-read":{"short":"<code>available()</code> says how many typed letters are waiting; <code>read()</code> takes the next one.","simple":"Checking the letterbox: <code>available()</code> tells you how many letters are in it, <code>read()</code> takes one out.","deep":"Both return immediately; <code>read()</code> gives -1 when nothing is waiting. The labs collect letters into a buffer until a newline, then run the command, so typing never blocks the screen. <code>flush()</code> waits until everything printed has been sent."},"pin-mode":{"short":"Sets a GPIO pin up as an input or an output before you use it.","simple":"Deciding whether a door is for coming in (reading a button) or going out (lighting an LED).","deep":"Configures the GPIO matrix for that pin. <code>INPUT_PULLUP</code> also turns on a weak internal resistor to 3.3 V, so a button to GND needs no extra parts. Avoid the strapping, flash and PSRAM pins listed at the top of <code>pins.h</code>."},"pin-modes":{"short":"The ways a pin can be set up: listen, drive, or listen with a built-in resistor.","simple":"<code>OUTPUT</code> is a tap you open and close. <code>INPUT</code> is a gauge you read. <code>INPUT_PULLUP</code> is a gauge that rests at \\"high\\" until something pulls it down.","deep":"A floating <code>INPUT</code> picks up noise; <code>INPUT_PULLUP</code>/<code>INPUT_PULLDOWN</code> enable the ESP32's weak internal resistors (tens of kilo-ohms). That's why pressed buttons read <code>LOW</code> in these labs."},"digital-write":{"short":"Switches an output pin on (3.3 V) or off (0 V).","simple":"Flicking a light switch: <code>HIGH</code> is on, <code>LOW</code> is off.","deep":"The ESP32-S3 pins are 3.3 V, never 5 V. Each pin can source or sink around 20 mA by default (adjustable with <code>gpio_set_drive_capability</code>), so an LED always needs a series resistor."},"digital-read":{"short":"Reads whether an input pin is high (near 3.3 V) or low (near 0 V).","simple":"Asking a doorbell button: are you pressed right now?","deep":"Returns <code>HIGH</code> (1) or <code>LOW</code> (0). With <code>INPUT_PULLUP</code> a pressed button reads <code>LOW</code>, hence <code>== LOW</code> meaning \\"pressed\\". Mechanical contacts bounce for a few ms, which <code>Button</code> filters out."},"high-low":{"short":"The two levels of a digital pin: <code>HIGH</code> is 3.3 V, <code>LOW</code> is 0 V.","simple":"On and off, voltage-style.","deep":"They are simply 1 and 0. On the ESP32 an input reads <code>HIGH</code> above roughly three quarters of 3.3 V and <code>LOW</code> below a quarter; in between is not guaranteed."},"analog-read":{"short":"Measures the voltage on a pin as a number from 0 to 4095.","simple":"A dimmer reading instead of an on/off switch: how far up is the voltage, on a scale of 0 to 4095?","deep":"The ESP32-S3 ADC is 12-bit. Use ADC1 pins (GPIO 1-10): ADC2 is shared with Wi-Fi. One call is one slow sample, which is why the sound labs use the ADC's continuous mode with DMA instead."},"millis":{"short":"How many milliseconds have passed since the board started.","simple":"A stopwatch that starts at power-on and never stops. Compare it with an earlier reading to know how long it's been.","deep":"Returns a 32-bit <code>unsigned long</code> derived from the 64-bit <code>esp_timer</code>. It wraps to 0 after about 49.7 days, and <code>millis() - last >= interval</code> stays correct across the wrap thanks to unsigned maths. This is how loops do things \\"every N ms\\" without <code>delay()</code>."},"micros":{"short":"How many microseconds (millionths of a second) have passed since the board started.","simple":"The same stopwatch as <code>millis()</code>, read a thousand times more finely.","deep":"32-bit, so it wraps after about 71.6 minutes; fine for timing short things, use <code>esp_timer_get_time()</code> for a 64-bit clock that never wraps in practice."},"delay":{"short":"Pauses the current code for a number of milliseconds.","simple":"Taking a nap: nothing else in <code>loop()</code> happens until you wake up.","deep":"On the ESP32 it calls <code>vTaskDelay</code>, so the CPU is free for other tasks (Wi-Fi, BLE, the sampler) while this one sleeps. The FreeRTOS tick is 1 ms here. Long delays still freeze buttons and the screen, so the labs mostly use <code>millis()</code> checks."},"delay-microseconds":{"short":"Waits a tiny number of microseconds, for hardware that needs very short pauses.","simple":"Holding your breath for a blink: too short to nap, so you just wait in place.","deep":"A busy-wait: the CPU spins and other tasks on this core don't run. Fine for a few hundred microseconds (letting a pull-up settle), never for long waits."},"ledc-attach":{"short":"Turns a pin into a PWM output with a chosen frequency and resolution, for dimming LEDs or backlights.","simple":"Setting up a very fast light switch that flicks on and off thousands of times a second, so the eye sees \\"half bright\\".","deep":"Arduino-ESP32 3.x API on the LEDC peripheral: <code>ledcAttach(pin, freq, bits)</code> returns <code>false</code> if no channel or frequency fits. 5 kHz at 8 bits gives duty values 0-255 with no visible flicker."},"ledc-write":{"short":"Sets how much of each PWM cycle the pin stays on: 0 is off, the top value is fully on.","simple":"Turning the dimmer knob on that fast switch.","deep":"In Arduino-ESP32 3.x it takes the pin (2.x took a channel). The duty range is 0 to 2^bits - 1 (0-255 for 8 bits) from <code>ledcAttach</code>; the LEDC hardware keeps the waveform running with no CPU work."},"rgb-led-write":{"short":"Sets the colour of the board's built-in RGB LED from red, green and blue values (0-255 each).","simple":"Mixing paint: a bit of red, lots of green, no blue. The LED shows the mix.","deep":"The on-board LED is a WS2812 that takes colour as a precisely timed pulse train; Arduino-ESP32 generates it with the RMT peripheral. It is very bright, so the labs scale values down. Was called <code>neopixelWrite</code> in older cores."},"attach-interrupt":{"short":"Runs a function the instant a pin changes, even while the rest of the code is busy.","simple":"A doorbell instead of peeking out the window: the board gets interrupted the moment someone presses.","deep":"<code>CHANGE</code>, <code>RISING</code> or <code>FALLING</code> chooses which edges fire. <code>attachInterruptArg</code> also passes a <code>void *</code> (here <code>this</code>) so a static handler can find its object. Handlers must be short, marked <code>IRAM_ATTR</code>, and only touch <code>volatile</code> or atomic data."},"digital-pin-to-interrupt":{"short":"Turns a pin number into the interrupt number <code>attachInterrupt</code> expects.","simple":"Looking up which doorbell wire belongs to which door.","deep":"On the ESP32 every GPIO can interrupt and the number is the pin itself, but writing it keeps code portable to boards where they differ."},"random":{"short":"Picks a whole number at random: <code>random(40, 200)</code> gives 40 up to 199.","simple":"Rolling dice whose sides you choose. The top number is never rolled.","deep":"<code>random(max)</code> gives 0 to max-1, <code>random(min, max)</code> min to max-1. <code>randomSeed(esp_random())</code> starts the sequence from a hardware random number, so every boot looks different."},"constrain":{"short":"Keeps a value between a lowest and a highest allowed value.","simple":"Bumpers on a bowling lane: the ball can roll anywhere between them, never past.","deep":"Arduino's version of <code>std::clamp</code>: <code>constrain(x, lo, hi)</code>. MIDI notes and velocities must stay 0-127, so the BLE MIDI lab clamps them."},"min-max":{"short":"Arduino's shorthand for the smaller or the larger of two values.","simple":"\\"Whichever is smaller\\" or \\"whichever is bigger\\". <code>max(2, s / 6)</code> means \\"at least 2\\".","deep":"Give both arguments the same type to avoid surprises. Elsewhere the labs write <code>std::min</code>/<code>std::max</code>, which also accept an explicit type: <code>std::min&lt;uint32_t&gt;(n, 320)</code>."},"string-c-str":{"short":"Gives a <code>String</code>'s text as a plain C string, for functions that want <code>const char *</code>.","simple":"Taking the letters out of their stretchy notebook and laying them on the table, for someone who only reads tiles.","deep":"The pointer is only valid while the <code>String</code> lives and isn't changed; never keep it from a temporary such as <code>a.toString().c_str()</code> beyond that line. Needed for <code>%s</code> in printf."},"string-is-empty":{"short":"Ask a <code>String</code> whether it has no letters, or how many letters it has.","simple":"Is the notebook page blank? How many letters are on it?","deep":"<code>length()</code> counts characters (bytes, so an emoji counts as several) and doesn't include the hidden <code>'\\\\0'</code>. <code>isEmpty()</code> is the same as <code>length() == 0</code> and reads better."},"string-substring":{"short":"<code>indexOf</code> finds where some text appears; <code>substring</code> cuts out a piece.","simple":"Finding a word in a sentence with your finger, then snipping out the part you need with scissors.","deep":"Positions count from 0. <code>substring(from)</code> goes to the end, <code>substring(from, to)</code> stops just before <code>to</code>. <code>indexOf</code> returns -1 when nothing is found. Each <code>substring</code> makes a new <code>String</code> on the heap."},"string-starts-with":{"short":"Checks whether a <code>String</code> begins (or ends) with some text.","simple":"\\"Does this command start with 'vol '?\\" A quick yes or no.","deep":"Case-sensitive, which is why commands are lower-cased first (<code>lower.startsWith(...)</code>). It's how every lab's serial command parser recognises its words."},"string-trim":{"short":"Tidy text in place: remove spaces at the ends, change letter case, or swap one piece of text for another.","simple":"Straightening a note before reading it: no stray spaces, all in small letters, so \\"Help \\" and \\"help\\" mean the same.","deep":"These change the <code>String</code> itself and return nothing, so call them on a copy you own: <code>String lower = line; lower.toLowerCase();</code>. <code>trim</code> also removes the <code>\\\\r</code> some terminals send."},"string-to-int":{"short":"Turns text like <code>\\"42\\"</code> into the number 42.","simple":"Reading the digits on a note and typing them into a calculator.","deep":"Returns a <code>long</code> (or <code>float</code>). Text that doesn't start with a number gives 0, so a typed <code>0</code> and nonsense look the same; check the text when that matters."},"spi-begin":{"short":"Starts the SPI bus on the pins you choose, so the board can talk fast to the screen or the RFID reader.","simple":"Laying a private phone line with separate wires for talking, listening and the beat both sides keep time to.","deep":"<code>SPI.begin(sck, miso, mosi, ss)</code>; -1 skips a pin (the display has no MISO). <code>SPI</code> is the S3's SPI2 (FSPI); the RFID track builds a second <code>SPIClass</code> on SPI3 so the two never share wires or timing."},"log-macros":{"short":"Print a tagged log line: <code>i</code> info, <code>w</code> warning, <code>e</code> error, <code>d</code> debug.","simple":"Notes in a ship's logbook, each with a stamp saying how serious it is.","deep":"Arduino-ESP32 macros with printf-style patterns. Each line is prefixed with level, file, line and function, and lines above <code>CORE_DEBUG_LEVEL</code> (3 = info in <code>platformio.ini</code>) are removed at compile time, so <code>log_d</code> costs nothing here. Output goes to the same serial port as <code>Serial</code>."},"esp-info":{"short":"Ask the chip about itself: model, cores, speed, flash and PSRAM size, MAC address.","simple":"The board reading its own ID card out loud.","deep":"Lesson 01 expects <i>ESP32-S3</i>, 2 cores at 240 MHz, 16 MB flash (the N16) and about 8 MB PSRAM (the R8). <code>getEfuseMac()</code> returns the factory MAC from eFuse as a <code>uint64_t</code>, lowest byte first."},"free-heap":{"short":"How much memory is still free for new allocations.","simple":"Checking how much room is left in the cupboard before buying more.","deep":"<code>ESP.getFreeHeap()</code> counts internal RAM; <code>heap_caps_get_free_size(MALLOC_CAP_SPIRAM)</code> counts PSRAM. Free is not the same as usable: <code>heap_caps_get_largest_free_block</code> tells you the biggest single piece, which is what a frame buffer needs."},"esp-restart":{"short":"Restarts the chip from scratch, as if you had pressed the reset button.","simple":"Switching it off and on again, from inside the program.","deep":"A software reset: RAM is cleared, NVS (Preferences) survives, and <code>esp_reset_reason()</code> reports <code>ESP_RST_SW</code> next time. Used after changing a saved setting that only applies at start-up."},"esp-reset-reason":{"short":"Tells you why the board last started: power-on, reset button, crash, brown-out...","simple":"Waking up and asking \\"did I just sleep, or did I fall over?\\"","deep":"Returns an <code>esp_reset_reason_t</code> such as <code>ESP_RST_POWERON</code>, <code>ESP_RST_SW</code>, <code>ESP_RST_PANIC</code>, <code>ESP_RST_TASK_WDT</code> or <code>ESP_RST_BROWNOUT</code> (supply voltage dipped). Printing it at boot makes crashes visible."},"esp-timer-get-time":{"short":"Microseconds since boot, from a 64-bit clock that never runs out.","simple":"A very precise stopwatch: good for measuring how long one screen frame took to draw.","deep":"Returns <code>int64_t</code> from the ESP-IDF high-resolution timer; safe to call from an interrupt. <code>millis()</code> and <code>micros()</code> are built on it. The button handler keeps only the low 32 bits so one read is a single step."},"esp-timer":{"short":"Sets up a callback that ESP-IDF runs after a delay or on a steady beat, with microsecond timing.","simple":"A kitchen timer that rings every 125 ms, whatever the rest of the code is doing.","deep":"Callbacks run in the high-priority <code>esp_timer</code> task, not in <code>loop()</code>, so keep them short and share data safely. Periods are in microseconds."},"esp-random":{"short":"A random 32-bit number from the chip's hardware random number generator.","simple":"Dice rolled by physics inside the chip rather than by a formula.","deep":"Truly random while Wi-Fi or Bluetooth is running (radio noise feeds it); before that, treat it as only pseudo-random. <code>esp_fill_random(buf, n)</code> fills a buffer, as the vault lab does for its secret key."},"heap-caps-malloc":{"short":"Asks for a block of memory from a chosen kind of RAM: fast internal or big external PSRAM.","simple":"Choosing between the small drawer at your desk (quick to reach) and the big storeroom down the hall (roomy but slower).","deep":"The S3 has about 512 KB of internal SRAM and here 8 MB of octal PSRAM. <code>MALLOC_CAP_INTERNAL</code> or <code>MALLOC_CAP_SPIRAM</code> picks the pool; the result is <code>nullptr</code> if no block is big enough. Free it with <code>heap_caps_free</code>."},"gpio-drive":{"short":"Chooses how strongly an output pin pushes current, from weak to strong.","simple":"Setting how hard a tap can push water: a gentle trickle or full pressure.","deep":"An ESP-IDF call (it needs a <code>gpio_num_t</code>, hence the cast). Levels 0-3 are roughly 5, 10, 20 (the default) and 40 mA. A weaker drive makes edges gentler and keeps LEDs dimmer."},"esp-err":{"short":"ESP-IDF functions answer with a result code; <code>ESP_OK</code> (0) means it worked.","simple":"A receipt: \\"OK\\", or a reason why not.","deep":"Any other value is an error such as <code>ESP_ERR_NO_MEM</code> or <code>ESP_ERR_TIMEOUT</code>; <code>esp_err_to_name()</code> turns it into text for the log. The labs check <code>!= ESP_OK</code> and bail out instead of crashing."},"preferences":{"short":"Saves small settings in the chip's flash so they survive power-off and re-flashing.","simple":"A notebook glued inside the board: write \\"Wi-Fi name = home\\" and it is still there tomorrow.","deep":"Arduino's wrapper around ESP-IDF NVS, a key-value store in its own flash partition. <code>begin(\\"ns\\", readOnly)</code> opens a namespace; namespaces and keys are at most 15 characters. Not encrypted by default; call <code>end()</code> when done."},"preferences-put-get":{"short":"Write a value under a key, or read it back with a fallback if it was never saved.","simple":"Labelled jars on a shelf: put something in the jar called \\"vol\\", and later take it out again.","deep":"The type is in the name (<code>UChar</code> = <code>uint8_t</code>, <code>UInt</code> = <code>uint32_t</code>). <code>getX(key, fallback)</code> returns the fallback when the key is missing. Writes cost flash wear, so the labs save only on change."},"x-task-create":{"short":"Starts a new task: a function that runs alongside <code>loop()</code>, on a core you choose.","simple":"Hiring a second cook. They have their own recipe and work at the same time as you, on their own stove.","deep":"Arguments: function, name, stack size, a <code>void *</code> parameter (often <code>this</code>), priority, an optional handle, core. In ESP-IDF the stack size is in <b>bytes</b>. <code>loop()</code> runs on core 1; the labs pin their readers and samplers to core 0. Returns <code>pdPASS</code> on success."},"v-task-delay":{"short":"Puts this task to sleep for a number of ticks, so other tasks get the CPU.","simple":"The second cook stepping back from the stove for a moment so someone else can use it.","deep":"Ticks are 1 ms on Arduino-ESP32, but write <code>pdMS_TO_TICKS(ms)</code> so the meaning is clear. A task's endless loop must block or delay somewhere, or lower-priority tasks starve and the watchdog complains."},"pd-ms-to-ticks":{"short":"Converts milliseconds into FreeRTOS ticks, the unit every wait in FreeRTOS uses.","simple":"Translating minutes into the kitchen clock's own units.","deep":"A macro: <code>ms * configTICK_RATE_HZ / 1000</code>. With the 1000 Hz tick here it is one to one, but the code stays right if the tick rate changes. Waits shorter than a tick round down to 0."},"port-max-delay":{"short":"\\"Wait forever\\": pass it as a timeout to block until something arrives.","simple":"Sitting by the letterbox until the post comes, however long it takes.","deep":"The largest tick count, which FreeRTOS treats as no timeout. Good for a worker task that has nothing else to do; never use it in <code>loop()</code> or an interrupt."},"pd-true":{"short":"FreeRTOS's own yes/no answers: <code>pdTRUE</code>/<code>pdPASS</code> mean it worked.","simple":"The receipt FreeRTOS hands back after a queue or task request.","deep":"Plain integers (<code>BaseType_t</code>), 1 for true/pass and 0 for false/fail. Compare against them explicitly: <code>xQueueSend(...) == pdTRUE</code>."},"x-queue-create":{"short":"Makes a queue: a safe mailbox for passing items from one task to another.","simple":"A post box with a fixed number of slots: one task drops letters in, another takes them out, and they never collide.","deep":"<code>xQueueCreate(length, itemSize)</code> returns a <code>QueueHandle_t</code>, or null if out of memory. Items are <b>copied</b> in and out, so send small structs, not pointers to things that might disappear."},"x-queue-send":{"short":"Puts a copy of an item into a queue, waiting up to a timeout if it is full.","simple":"Dropping a letter in the post box. If the box is full you can wait a moment, or walk away.","deep":"A timeout of 0 never blocks and returns <code>errQUEUE_FULL</code> when there's no room: right for <code>loop()</code> and web handlers, which must not stall. From an interrupt use the <code>FromISR</code> version."},"x-queue-receive":{"short":"Takes the next item out of a queue, waiting up to a timeout if it is empty.","simple":"Opening the post box: take the oldest letter, or wait a bit for one to arrive.","deep":"Items come out in order, copied into your variable. A worker task typically blocks here with a timeout (or <code>portMAX_DELAY</code>), using no CPU until work arrives."},"semaphore":{"short":"A signal one task waits for and another (or an interrupt) gives.","simple":"A baton in a relay race: the runner waits until the baton is handed over, then goes.","deep":"The MFRC522 driver sleeps in <code>xSemaphoreTake(done_, timeout)</code> until the reader's IRQ interrupt calls <code>xSemaphoreGiveFromISR</code>, so no SPI polling is needed. A mutex semaphore adds priority inheritance for locking."},"task-handle":{"short":"A handle names a running task; <code>vTaskDelete</code> ends one.","simple":"The second cook's name badge, and the note that sends them home.","deep":"<code>vTaskDelete(nullptr)</code> ends the calling task; a task function must never just return. <code>uxTaskGetStackHighWaterMark</code> shows how close a task came to overflowing its stack."},"critical-section":{"short":"Locks a few lines so no other task, core or interrupt can touch the same data meanwhile.","simple":"Holding the door shut with your foot for a second while you swap two things on the shelf.","deep":"Takes a <code>portMUX_TYPE</code> spinlock and disables interrupts on this core, so it works across both ESP32 cores and with interrupts. Keep the section to a few copies: no printing, no waiting, no allocation."},"adafruit-st7789":{"short":"The driver for the 1.9-inch 320×170 colour screen used from lesson 03.","simple":"The interpreter between the board and the screen: you say \\"draw this\\", it speaks the screen's language over SPI.","deep":"Adafruit's ST7789 driver. <code>init(170, 320)</code> sets the panel size, <code>setRotation(1)</code> turns it landscape, <code>setSPISpeed</code> pushes the clock up to 40 MHz. Pixels are RGB565."},"adafruit-gfx":{"short":"The drawing library behind every screen call: lines, circles, rectangles, text.","simple":"A box of stencils and pens. Any screen that can colour one pixel gets all the other shapes for free.","deep":"An abstract base class: a subclass only has to implement <code>drawPixel</code>, and everything else is built on it (our <code>FrameBuffer</code> also overrides the line and fill calls for speed). Coordinates are <code>int16_t</code>, from the top-left corner."},"gfx-canvas16":{"short":"An off-screen picture in memory that you draw on, then send to the screen in one go.","simple":"Sketching on paper first and then holding the finished page up to the window, so nobody sees half a drawing.","deep":"Adafruit GFX's 16-bit canvas: a <code>uint16_t</code> per pixel, 320×170×2 = about 106 KB. It allocates with plain <code>malloc</code>, which here lands in PSRAM; our <code>FrameBuffer</code> prefers internal RAM instead."},"frame-buffer":{"short":"This project's off-screen picture: draw into it with the normal GFX calls, then push it to the screen.","simple":"Our own sketch pad, kept in the fastest drawer that has room.","deep":"Defined in <code>kit/lib/bench/src/frame_buffer.h</code>. <code>begin()</code> tries fast internal RAM first and falls back to PSRAM, and <code>internal()</code> says which it got. It overrides <code>drawPixel</code>, <code>fillScreen</code> and the line and rectangle calls with direct writes into <code>pixels()</code>."},"display":{"short":"This project's screen helper: the ST7789, its backlight and the frame buffer, set up the same way in every lab.","simple":"The screen and its sketch pad packaged together: draw on <code>fb()</code>, then <code>push()</code> shows it.","deep":"Defined in <code>kit/lib/bench/src/display.h</code>. <code>begin()</code> starts PWM on the backlight, SPI at 40 MHz and the panel in landscape; <code>push()</code> sends the whole picture with one <code>drawRGBBitmap</code>."},"button":{"short":"This project's push-button helper: turns raw presses into clean clicks, double-clicks and long presses.","simple":"A doorman for the button: it ignores the jitter of the metal contacts and tells you what the person really meant.","deep":"Defined in <code>kit/lib/bench/src/button.h</code>. An <code>IRAM_ATTR</code> interrupt counts every edge; <code>poll()</code> accepts a new level after 25 ms of quiet and reports <code>Event::Click</code>, <code>DoubleClick</code> (within 280 ms) or <code>LongPress</code> (600 ms)."},"lab-shell":{"short":"The shared shell each track's labs are built on: screen, button, LED, serial commands and hooks.","simple":"A ready-built workbench. Each lab only brings its own tools (views and commands) and plugs them in.","deep":"A lab is a <code>...Spec</code>: its views (draw functions plus a double-click action), its serial commands and hooks such as <code>setup</code>, <code>tick</code> and <code>gesture</code>. <code>lab.warn</code> and <code>lab.note</code> print printf-style messages to the log and the screen. Each <code>src/&lt;lab&gt;/main.cpp</code> is mostly that list, so <code>help</code> shows exactly what the lesson teaches."},"mfrc522":{"short":"This project's own driver for the RC522 card reader, written register by register with no library.","simple":"Speaking to the reader chip directly, one byte at a time, so you can see every word of the conversation.","deep":"Defined in <code>kit/lib/rfid-kit/src/mfrc522.h</code>: SPI framing, registers, FIFO, the chip's timer and either polling or sleeping on its IRQ wire. <code>iso14443a.h</code> and <code>mifare_classic.h</code> build the card protocol on top."},"rgb565":{"short":"Turn red, green and blue (or a hue on the colour wheel) into one 16-bit screen colour.","simple":"Mixing paint, then writing the mix as a single code the screen understands.","deep":"RGB565 keeps 5 bits of red, 6 of green and 5 of blue: <code>((r &amp; 0xF8) &lt;&lt; 8) | ((g &amp; 0xFC) &lt;&lt; 3) | (b &gt;&gt; 3)</code>. Our <code>rgb()</code> is <code>constexpr</code>, so palette colours cost nothing at runtime."},"gfx-fill-screen":{"short":"Paints the whole screen (or canvas) one colour.","simple":"A fresh coat of paint over everything, usually the first step of drawing a new frame.","deep":"On our <code>FrameBuffer</code> it's a single <code>std::fill_n</code> over 54 400 pixels; on the panel itself it streams that many pixels over SPI."},"gfx-draw-pixel":{"short":"Colours one single dot at x, y.","simple":"One dot of a felt-tip pen.","deep":"The one function every Adafruit GFX surface must provide; all other shapes can be made from it. Ours checks the bounds and writes <code>buf_[y * _width + x]</code>."},"gfx-rect":{"short":"Draw a rectangle from its top-left corner, width and height: filled, outlined, or with rounded corners.","simple":"Stamping a box shape onto the picture.","deep":"Arguments: <code>(x, y, w, h, colour)</code>, plus a corner radius for the round versions. <code>fill</code> paints the inside, <code>draw</code> only the outline. Bars, panels and buttons on every lab screen are made of these."},"gfx-circle":{"short":"Draw round shapes and triangles: circles from a centre and radius, triangles from three corners.","simple":"A compass and a set square for the screen.","deep":"<code>fillCircle(cx, cy, r, colour)</code> uses the centre, not a corner. Triangles take <code>(x0, y0, x1, y1, x2, y2, colour)</code>. All built from fast horizontal lines on our frame buffer."},"gfx-line":{"short":"Draw a straight line between two points, or a fast horizontal or vertical one.","simple":"A ruler and pencil. The \\"fast\\" ones only go straight across or straight down, and are much quicker.","deep":"<code>drawLine(x0, y0, x1, y1, c)</code> uses Bresenham's algorithm, one pixel at a time. <code>drawFastHLine(x, y, w, c)</code> fills a run of pixels with one <code>std::fill_n</code> on our frame buffer, so dividers and bars use it."},"gfx-cursor":{"short":"<code>setCursor</code> sets where text starts; <code>fb.print</code> then writes text there on the screen.","simple":"Putting the pen down at a spot before writing.","deep":"With the built-in font the point is the text's top-left; with a custom <code>GFXfont</code> it is the left end of the baseline, so y must be lower. <code>print</code> and <code>printf</code> (same <code>%</code> codes as <code>Serial.printf</code>) move the cursor along as they write."},"gfx-text-style":{"short":"Choose the colour, size and font of the text you print next.","simple":"Picking which pen and which lettering stencil to use.","deep":"<code>setTextColor(fg)</code> draws transparent text; <code>setTextColor(fg, bg)</code> also paints the background (built-in font only). <code>setFont(&amp;FreeSans9pt7b)</code> picks a smooth font that ships with Adafruit GFX (9 pt, regular); <code>setFont(nullptr)</code> returns to the 6×8 built-in font; <code>setTextSize(2)</code> doubles it."},"gfx-text-bounds":{"short":"Measures how wide and tall some text would be, without drawing it.","simple":"Measuring a sign before hanging it, so you can centre it on the wall.","deep":"<code>getTextBounds(text, x, y, &amp;x1, &amp;y1, &amp;w, &amp;h)</code> fills in the box the text would cover with the current font and size; used to centre and right-align labels."},"gfx-rgb-bitmap":{"short":"Copies a whole block of pixels onto the screen in one go.","simple":"Sliding a finished photo into the frame, instead of painting it dot by dot.","deep":"<code>drawRGBBitmap(x, y, pixels, w, h)</code> streams RGB565 data over SPI; a full 320×170 frame is about 106 KB, roughly 22 ms at 40 MHz. That is the frame rate's upper limit, which is why the labs draw off-screen first."},"gfx-rotation":{"short":"Turns the screen's picture in steps of 90°, so it reads the right way up.","simple":"Turning a photo frame from portrait to landscape.","deep":"0-3 are 0°, 90°, 180°, 270°. Rotation 1 makes the 170×320 panel a 320×170 landscape screen; width and height swap accordingly."},"wifi-begin":{"short":"Starts joining a Wi-Fi network with its name and password.","simple":"Knocking on the router's door with the password; the answer comes a few seconds later.","deep":"Returns at once; joining happens in the background Wi-Fi task. Our <code>Connection</code> class listens for the events (connected, got IP, disconnected) instead of waiting in a loop. Passing <code>nullptr</code> as the password joins an open network."},"wifi-mode":{"short":"Chooses whether the board joins a network (station), makes its own (access point), or both.","simple":"Being a guest at someone else's party, hosting your own, or doing both at once.","deep":"<code>WIFI_AP_STA</code> runs both on one radio, so they share a channel; the setup portal uses it to show a page while it tries your network. <code>softAP</code> starts the board's own network, reachable at <code>192.168.4.1</code> by default."},"wifi-info":{"short":"Ask the Wi-Fi connection for details: the board's IP address, signal strength, channel, MAC.","simple":"Reading the board's house number on the network, and how many bars of signal it has.","deep":"<code>localIP()</code> is an <code>IPAddress</code> (<code>toString()</code> for text). <code>RSSI()</code> is in dBm: about -50 is excellent, below -80 is weak. All are 0 or empty until connected."},"wifi-scan":{"short":"Listens for nearby Wi-Fi networks and lists their names, signal and security.","simple":"Turning the radio dial to hear which stations are around.","deep":"With <code>async = true</code> it returns at once (-1, still running); poll <code>scanComplete()</code> for the count, read each entry, then <code>scanDelete()</code> to free the list. A scan takes a few seconds across all channels."},"wifi-events":{"short":"Registers a function the Wi-Fi driver calls when something happens: connected, got an IP, dropped off.","simple":"Asking the receptionist to ring you when your visitor arrives, instead of waiting in the lobby.","deep":"The callback runs in the Wi-Fi event task, not in <code>loop()</code>, so it should only set flags; <code>loop()</code> does the real work. <code>info</code> carries details such as the disconnect reason."},"mdns":{"short":"Gives the board a friendly name like <code>bench.local</code> on your network.","simple":"A name tag, so other devices can find the board without knowing its number.","deep":"Multicast DNS answers name queries on the local network only; macOS and iOS support it out of the box. <code>addService(\\"http\\", \\"tcp\\", 80)</code> also advertises the web server."},"ntp-time":{"short":"Sets the clock from internet time servers, then reads the local date and time.","simple":"The board has no battery-backed clock, so it asks the internet \\"what time is it?\\" once it's online.","deep":"<code>configTzTime(tz, server1, server2)</code> sets the POSIX time zone and starts SNTP. <code>getLocalTime(&amp;tm, timeoutMs)</code> fills a <code>struct tm</code> and returns <code>false</code> until the first sync has happened."},"http-client":{"short":"Makes web requests from the board, like a tiny browser: ask a URL, get the answer back.","simple":"Sending a letter to a website and waiting for its reply.","deep":"Arduino-ESP32's HTTP client over a TCP or TLS connection. Our <code>https::get</code> checks the server's certificate against the bundled Mozilla roots. It blocks for 0.3 s to several seconds, so call it from a worker task, never from <code>loop()</code>."},"json-document":{"short":"ArduinoJson's container for JSON data: fill it like a dictionary, or read one you received.","simple":"A filing tray with labelled folders: <code>doc[\\"heap\\"] = 1234</code> puts a number in the folder called heap.","deep":"ArduinoJson 7: a <code>JsonDocument</code> grows on the heap as needed. <code>doc[\\"k\\"].to&lt;JsonArray&gt;()</code> makes nested arrays; <code>doc[\\"k\\"] | fallback</code> reads with a default if the key is missing or the wrong type."},"serialize-json":{"short":"Turns a <code>JsonDocument</code> into JSON text, ready to send.","simple":"Typing up the filing tray's contents into one tidy letter.","deep":"<code>serializeJson(doc, out)</code> writes into a <code>String</code>, a <code>char</code> buffer or a stream such as <code>Serial</code>, and returns the number of bytes written."},"deserialize-json":{"short":"Reads JSON text into a <code>JsonDocument</code> you can look things up in.","simple":"Opening a letter and sorting its contents into the labelled folders.","deep":"Returns a <code>DeserializationError</code> that is true when something went wrong (<code>e.c_str()</code> says what). A <code>Filter</code> keeps only the fields you need, which saves a lot of RAM on big weather replies."},"async-web-server":{"short":"A web server on the board: your phone or laptop opens a page or API, and the board answers.","simple":"A tiny shop window: each address (<code>/api/card</code>) has a clerk who knows what to reply.","deep":"ESPAsyncWebServer answers in its own network task, so the screen and the reader never wait. <code>server_.on(path, HTTP_GET, handler)</code> registers a route; handlers must be quick and must not block, so heavy work is queued to another task."},"async-request":{"short":"One incoming web request: what was asked for, and how to answer it.","simple":"The customer at the shop window: you read their order and hand back a reply.","deep":"Handlers receive it as a pointer, hence <code>req-&gt;send(200, \\"application/json\\", body)</code>. <code>hasParam</code>/<code>getParam</code> read query or form fields. Every request must get exactly one <code>send</code>."},"async-web-socket":{"short":"A live two-way line to the browser, so the board can push updates the moment something happens.","simple":"A phone call instead of letters: the line stays open and either side can speak any time.","deep":"Mounted on the web server at a path such as <code>/ws</code>. <code>textAll</code> sends to every connected browser; <code>onEvent</code> hears connects, disconnects and messages. Call <code>cleanupClients()</code> now and then to drop connections that went away."},"nimble-device":{"short":"The front door to Bluetooth LE: starts the radio stack and hands out servers, scanners and clients.","simple":"The Bluetooth reception desk: say hello once with <code>init</code>, then ask it for whatever you need.","deep":"NimBLE-Arduino, a lighter BLE stack than Bluedroid. Its functions are <code>static</code>, so they are called as <code>NimBLEDevice::...</code> with no object. <code>init(name)</code> sets the advertised name; <code>setPower</code> sets transmit power in dBm."},"nimble-server":{"short":"The board's own Bluetooth menu: services group characteristics, small values a phone can read, write or subscribe to.","simple":"A noticeboard with labelled slots: a phone walks up, reads the temperature slot, or pins a message in the message slot.","deep":"GATT: a service (a UUID) holds characteristics, each with a UUID, properties such as <code>READ | WRITE | NOTIFY</code> and a value up to the MTU. <code>notify()</code> pushes a new value to subscribed phones without being asked."},"nimble-advertising":{"short":"Makes the board shout \\"I'm here!\\" a few times a second, so phones can see and connect to it.","simple":"A lighthouse: short flashes at regular intervals that anyone nearby can spot.","deep":"A legacy advertising packet carries at most 31 bytes (name, flags, service UUIDs, manufacturer data), plus 31 more in a scan response."},"nimble-scan":{"short":"Listens for other Bluetooth devices advertising nearby and reports each one found.","simple":"Watching the coastline for lighthouse flashes and writing down each one you see.","deep":"Each <code>NimBLEAdvertisedDevice</code> has an address, RSSI and payload. Active scanning also asks for scan responses. Callbacks run in the NimBLE host task, so hand results to <code>loop()</code> rather than drawing there."},"nimble-client":{"short":"The board as the visitor: connects to another Bluetooth device and reads or writes its values.","simple":"Walking up to someone else's noticeboard instead of hosting your own.","deep":"The central role. Connect, discover services (<code>getServices(true)</code>), then read, write or subscribe to remote characteristics. Connecting and discovery block, so B8 does them in a worker task."},"nimble-conn-info":{"short":"Details of one Bluetooth connection: who's connected, how it's secured, how big a packet can be.","simple":"The visitor's badge: name, whether they showed ID, and how much they can carry at once.","deep":"Passed to callbacks such as <code>onConnect</code> and <code>onAuthenticationComplete</code>: the peer's address, connection handle, MTU, and whether the link is encrypted, authenticated and bonded."},"nimble-callbacks":{"short":"Classes you inherit from to hear about Bluetooth events: connects, disconnects, writes, reads.","simple":"Leaving your phone number with the reception desk: \\"call me when someone arrives\\".","deep":"Override only the <code>virtual</code> functions you need (<code>onConnect</code>, <code>onWrite</code>...), then register the object with <code>setCallbacks</code>. They run in the NimBLE host task, so keep them short and thread-safe."},"nimble-security":{"short":"Decides how a Bluetooth link is secured: pairing, passkeys and remembered bonds.","simple":"The door policy: let anyone in, ask for a code, or only let in friends you've met before.","deep":"<code>setSecurityAuth(bond, mitm, sc)</code> asks for bonding, man-in-the-middle protection and LE Secure Connections; the IO capability decides whether a passkey or a yes/no comparison is shown. Bonds are stored in NVS and survive restarts."},"ble-note":{"short":"This project's shortcut for adding a line to the Bluetooth labs' activity log.","simple":"A quick jot in the lab notebook that the screen and serial log both show.","deep":"A macro from <code>kit/lib/ble-kit/src/ble_link.h</code> that expands to <code>::ble::activity().add(::ble::Act::kind, ...)</code>: a printf-style entry in a ring buffer guarded by a critical section, so callbacks in the NimBLE task can write safely."},"i2s-channel":{"short":"Sets up an I2S sound channel to a digital microphone or speaker amplifier.","simple":"Laying a conveyor belt for sound: a clock keeps the beat, and one number rides along for every sample.","deep":"ESP-IDF 5's I2S driver: <code>i2s_new_channel</code> creates TX and/or RX channels, <code>i2s_channel_init_std_mode</code> sets the sample rate, bit width and pins (standard Philips I2S format), <code>i2s_channel_enable</code> starts the clocks and DMA."},"i2s-read-write":{"short":"Takes samples from the microphone, or hands samples to the speaker, through the I2S channel.","simple":"Picking parcels off the conveyor belt, or putting them on it.","deep":"<code>i2s_channel_read(handle, buf, bytes, &amp;got, timeoutMs)</code> copies from the DMA buffers, waiting if none are ready. Writes wait while the DMA is full, so a speaker task runs exactly as fast as the sound plays."}}`),topics:{basics:{title:`Building blocks`,blurb:`The small symbols that do the sums, comparisons and choices in every line.`},types:{title:`Kinds of values`,blurb:`Whole numbers, decimals, text and yes/no: what each box can hold.`},flow:{title:`Making decisions & repeating`,blurb:`How the code chooses a path, loops, and finishes a job.`},functions:{title:`Functions`,blurb:`Named recipes the code can run, from setup() and loop() to tiny lambdas.`},memory:{title:`Memory & const`,blurb:`Where values live, how to point at them, and how to keep them from changing.`},classes:{title:`Types you build`,blurb:`Structs, classes, enums and namespaces: your own kinds of things.`},modern:{title:`Modern C++`,blurb:`Compile-time constants, templates, casts and the standard library's helpers.`},preprocessor:{title:`Before compiling`,blurb:`Lines starting with # that rearrange the code before the compiler reads it.`},arduino:{title:`Arduino basics`,blurb:`Pins, timing, serial printing and text: the everyday Arduino toolkit.`},esp:{title:`The ESP32 chip`,blurb:`Logging, memory, timers and storage that are special to this chip.`},rtos:{title:`Doing two things at once`,blurb:`FreeRTOS tasks, queues and locks that let the board multitask safely.`},libraries:{title:`Libraries`,blurb:`The screen, Wi-Fi, web server, JSON, Bluetooth and sound helpers the labs use.`}}};export{e as default};
